+## 5. Current Behavior
+
+```text
+model Tool input
+  -> ApplyPatchTool parses patchText
+  -> Patch.deriveNewContentsFromChunks
+  -> computeReplacements
+  -> seekSequence: exact -> rstrip -> trim -> Unicode-normalized whole-line windows
+  -> immediate generic throw when no whole-line window matches
+  -> ApplyPatchTool catches the throw
+  -> extractActualExcerpt scans the whole file using the first non-empty old line
+     from the first hunk chunk, even when a later chunk actually failed
+  -> unbounded character-set score selects a line
+  -> Tool reports that line as "Actual content near expected location"
+```
+
+`Patch.deriveNewContentsFromChunks` is also consumed by
+`Patch.applyHunksToFiles`, `Patch.applyPatch`, and
+`Patch.maybeParseApplyPatchVerified`. A repair only in `ApplyPatchTool` would
+leave the owning Patch semantics and the other consumers inconsistent.
+
+For `edit`:
+
+```text
+EditTool.execute
+  -> normalize line endings
+  -> replace
+  -> SimpleReplacer first yields the literal oldString
+  -> a unique substring succeeds and preserves surrounding characters
+  -> later supported replacers handle existing whitespace/indent/escape cases
+  -> only a true not-found calls findClosestMatch
+  -> first non-empty expected line + duplicated character-set score selects a line
+```
+
+Therefore the user's `ABCDEFG` / `CDEFG` replacement already succeeds through
+`edit`; duplicating that success path would be incorrect. The missing success
+behavior is in Patch chunk resolution. Both Tools still share the faulty
+diagnostic concept.
+
+The local database inventory used exact JSON Tool fields rather than a broad
+`part.data LIKE` scan, so analysis scripts and user messages quoting the error
+were excluded. It also counted failures in `state.output`: 12 completed
+`apply_patch` calls contained 17 failed hunks due to per-file partial success.
