+## 10. Single Approved Primary-Path Design
+
+The one semantic path is **ordered exact transformation of an atomic in-memory
+working copy**. Exact whole-line Patch matching and exact-substring Patch
+matching are ordered branches within this one contract, not independent
+implementations that race or catch-and-succeed. R2 resolves audit B-01 by
+removing every nonliteral Patch success branch after exact line matching. R8
+also removes the proposed original-offset/reverse-span representation that
+created R5-R7 delimiter-composition failures.
+
+```text
+patchText
+  -> parse ordered chunks
+  -> create LF-normalized working lines and cursorOffset in their current text
+  -> for every chunk, resolve any @@ context and establish its eligible lower
+     bound before classifying insertion versus replacement
+  -> for each non-insertion chunk, resolve and immediately apply the complete
+     parsed old block to the working copy:
+       1. try exact whole-line equality only (with current EOF end preference)
+       2. if no exact line window resolves, search the complete old block as an exact
+           literal substring inside the eligible current working text
+       3. accept only one eligible occurrence and replace only its character range
+       4. if zero or multiple eligible exact substring occurrences remain, fail
+  -> after every chunk validates, append deferred pure-insertion lines in patch order
+  -> preserve BOM and original line-ending style
+  -> produce the existing diff/permission/write/events result
+```
+
+`seekSequence` becomes exact-only; `normalizeUnicode` and the rstrip/trim
+comparators are deleted from Patch matching. The trailing-empty retry is also
+deleted: an explicit final empty element contributes the literal `"\n"` to the
+complete pattern and may not be removed after a miss.
+
+Edit independently converges on the same literal-only contract at its owner:
+
+```text
+oldString
+  -> exact literal occurrence count in current content
+  -> zero: diagnostic error only
+  -> one: replace that exact span
+  -> multiple + replaceAll: replace every exact span
+  -> multiple without replaceAll: explicit ambiguity error
+```
+
+`LineTrimmedReplacer`, `BlockAnchorReplacer`,
+`WhitespaceNormalizedReplacer`, `IndentationFlexibleReplacer`,
+`EscapeNormalizedReplacer`, `TrimmedBoundaryReplacer`,
+`ContextAwareReplacer`, their thresholds/Levenshtein support, and the redundant
+`MultiOccurrenceReplacer` generator are deleted. Repository search found no
+consumer outside `edit.ts`; the public Tool behavior is protected at the agreed
+Tool seam. This resolves R2 B-01 rather than letting exact literal search be
+followed by competing nonliteral success.
+
+The exact substring is `pattern.join("\n")`, not one selected line, and its
+replacement is `newSlice.join("\n")`. `deriveNewContentsFromChunks` separates
+the BOM and creates one canonical LF-normalized `workingLines` copy with the
+existing trailing terminator removed. No filesystem write occurs while chunks
+are processed; a later failure discards the local working copy and preserves
+the existing per-file atomicity contract.
+
+For each non-insertion chunk, the owner derives `workingText` by joining the
+current lines and resolves against that current value:
+
+- Exact whole-line success mutates `workingLines` with the existing logical-line
+  splice semantics. This directly preserves first/middle/final/only and
+  multiline deletion behavior without assigning delimiters to character spans.
+- Exact substring success splices only `[startOffset, endOffset)` in
+  `workingText`, then splits the result back into canonical working lines. It
+  preserves every prefix/suffix character, including multiline boundaries.
+- `cursorOffset` is expressed in the newly transformed working text. After a
+  non-empty line or substring replacement it points immediately after the
+  replacement; after deletion it points at the surviving boundary. Thus later
+  chunks cannot match newly inserted replacement text but may match surviving
+  text farther right on the same line.
+- Every search counts occurrences only at or after current `cursorOffset`.
+  Earlier text is consumed/ineligible; multiple later substring occurrences
+  remain ambiguous.
+- No transformation is published until all chunks have resolved. Sequential
+  mutation is local validation state, not partial Tool/file success.
+
+Pure insertion remains the parser's special EOF line-addition branch. It has no
+old block to match, but any `change_context` is resolved first through the same
+exact-line/unique-substring/failure contract and advances the current cursor as
+today. Only after that validation are its `new_lines` collected in patch order;
+all collected lines are appended once to final `workingLines`. This deliberately
+keeps newly added EOF text outside later searches and preserves current
+reverse-line-splice semantics. It also means:
+
+- non-empty lines plus `beta` become separate lines without manual LF ownership;
+- an empty working file plus `beta` starts directly with `beta`;
+- multiple insertion chunks retain patch order; and
+- deleting every original line before deferred insertion yields `beta`, not an
+  inherited leading blank line.
+
+Existing final-empty-line insertion, line-ending conversion, and BOM join then
+preserve empty-file, LF, CRLF, CR, and no-trailing-newline contracts.
+
+`is_end_of_file` retains its current preference only for the exact whole-line
+branch. It does not waive substring uniqueness: the literal substring branch
+counts every occurrence at or after current `cursorOffset`, rejects multiple
+occurrences even when one ends at EOF, and accepts one occurrence wherever the
+current forward-search contract permits it. This removes the R1 audit ambiguity
+between EOF preference and the user's non-whole-line unique-only rule.
+
+Before classifying or resolving any chunk, `change_context` uses the same
+private exact locator:
+
+```text
+change_context from current working-text cursorOffset
+  -> exact whole-line location: use that line
+  -> otherwise one exact literal substring: use its containing line
+  -> zero or multiple literal substring locations: context failure
+  -> advance old-block cursorOffset to the current start of the line after context
+```
+
+The context branch never replaces characters. It only derives the next
+eligible current working line, and it emits a context-specific owner error and
+candidate diagnostic before identity is lost. The exact locator is shared with
+old-block resolution; `ApplyPatchTool` receives no separate context
+fallback.
+
+The resolver returns an explicit no-match or ambiguous result rather than
+silently choosing. Multiple literal occurrences remain a failure and tell the
+caller to add context.
+
+For diagnostics, `patch/match.ts` provides one bounded, order-sensitive closest
+window calculation used by both Patch failure construction and Edit not-found
+rendering. Patch always supplies the immutable persisted file text retained at
+owner entry, not the prospective working copy, so a reported line remains true
+after the failed in-memory transformation is discarded:
+
+- Compare the complete expected block with candidate windows of the same
+  logical-line count across the persisted file. This diagnostic-only global scan
+  does not alter the ordered eligible range used for replacement success.
+- Normalize line endings and trim comparison indentation without changing the
+  raw excerpt.
+- Score Unicode code-point **bigram multisets** with
+  `2 * multisetIntersection / (expectedBigrams + candidateBigrams)`. This is
+  linear, bounded to `[0,1]`, retains local order and repeated-character weight,
+  and penalizes long unrelated candidates.
+- A zero-bigram expected/candidate naturally scores zero; short punctuation
+  cannot win through inflated overlap.
+- A single reliability gate suppresses the candidate when the best score is
+  below `0.5` or tied. The error
+  then explicitly says no reliable nearby candidate was found and instructs a
+  read, rather than labeling unrelated text as actual content.
+- Return the candidate's true one-based start line. Render candidate text first;
+  optional surrounding context is separately labeled. A 500-character budget
+  uses explicit head/tail omission around the candidate rather than silently
+  slicing from the preceding line.
+
+Patch constructs the failure while it still has the exact failed context or
+chunk and immutable persisted text. `ApplyPatchTool` removes
+`extractActualExcerpt` and only aggregates that owner error. Edit removes
+`charOverlap`/its duplicate scan and delegates
+candidate selection to the same pure matcher; its surrounding Tool
+read/permission/write/LSP/event pipeline remains unchanged, while its
+replacement resolver is explicitly simplified as described above.
+
+This repairs the first divergences: successful substring resolution occurs in
+the Patch owner for every consumer, and diagnostic evidence is produced before
+failed-chunk identity is lost.
