+## 20. Real Risks and Open Decisions
+
+### Confirmed Risks
+
+- Splitting a multiline character-splice result back into canonical working
+  lines can drop or duplicate boundary empties if trailing logical rows are
+  confused with the final file terminator. Owner tests use independent literal
+  outputs for prefix, suffix, deletion, blank lines, and multiline replacement.
+- Searching outside the current chunk cursor can reorder chunks or edit an
+  earlier duplicate. The resolver must receive the existing lower bound.
+- A simple denominator correction is insufficient: in stable local replays,
+  `common / candidate.length` selected a <=10-character line in 5/8 Edit and
+  6/13 Patch cases. The full order-sensitive matcher is required.
+- A diagnostic generated after the Patch error crosses into the Tool wrapper
+  loses failed-chunk identity. Error construction must remain in the owner.
+- A candidate rendered from the prospective working copy would not exist in the
+  unchanged file after failure. Patch diagnostics therefore score the retained
+  persisted input while owner replacement matching alone uses current text.
+- Tool partial success stores failed hunk text in a completed output. Existing
+  aggregation must preserve the richer owner error in both completed and error
+  states.
+- Removing Unicode-normalized Patch success is an intentional behavior change
+  required by the user's exact-only ordering. The prior success test must become
+  a failure-and-unchanged regression rather than be silently deleted.
+- Removing trailing-empty shortening is the same exact-only requirement, not a
+  newline-format regression: an explicit parsed empty old line must remain part
+  of both exact search patterns.
+- Commit hooks may reject or modify files. They must not be bypassed. Any
+  task-file mutation after the clean implementation audit invalidates the diff
+  identity and requires verification plus a new full-scope implementation audit
+  before another commit attempt.
+- Removing Edit's nonliteral replacers is the corresponding intentional half of
+  the dual-Tool exact-only contract. Existing literal unique, ambiguity, and
+  `replaceAll` outcomes remain; normalized success does not.
+- Context substring location must not consume or modify the context line. An
+  off-by-one lower bound would update the wrong occurrence or re-match context;
+  owner and Tool tests lock the following-line behavior.
+- `cursorOffset` is always recomputed in current working-text coordinates after
+  local mutation. Reusing pre-mutation offsets shifts later chunks; same-line
+  and generated-text-exclusion characterizations protect this boundary.
+- Exact-line mutation and exact-substring mutation intentionally use different
+  representations inside one owner path: line splice preserves logical rows,
+  while character splice preserves partial-line prefix/suffix text.
+- Pure insertion lines are withheld from matching and appended after every
+  ordinary chunk validates. Appending earlier would expose generated text;
+  deriving separators from original text regresses delete-all composition.
+- Every insertion chunk still validates its context before deferral. Moving the
+  insertion classification above context lookup would turn `@@ missing` from a
+  failure into an unrelated EOF append.
